+### [Per-Point Normal Lighting](14918-per-point-normal-lighting.md)
+Per-Point Normal Lighting is a volume rendering technique that calculates the surface normal vector at each individual resampling point along the ray and substitutes this normal directly into the lighting model. This approach solves the problem of visual flatness and loss of microstructural detail inherent in methods that interpolate lighting results without computing per-sample normals. By computing normals at the sampling points, the method accurately represents diffuse and specular reflections, preserving the three-dimensional sense and concavity/convexity of the data field.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: The method adds three additional texture lookups and basic vector operations per shaded voxel [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity**: No additional memory is required beyond the volume texture and standard uniforms [S1].
-*   **Performance**: Fetching data from neighbor voxels and performing extra calculations increases rendering cost, potentially impacting performance [S1].
-*   **Strengths**: Simple to implement; works with any scalar field; provides immediate visual improvement in depth perception and shape definition [S1].
-*   **Limitations**: Incoherent results with transparent transfer functions; sensitive to scalar field noise [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds a constant number of texture lookups (3) and vector operations per sample. This increases the computational cost per sample compared to unlit rendering, potentially impacting performance in real-time applications [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory structures are required beyond the scalar volume texture and standard uniforms [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** It provides a simple and effective way to add depth cues and surface detail to volume renderings without requiring explicit surface extraction or complex shading models [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** It is sensitive to the resolution of the volume data; low-resolution data may result in blocky or inaccurate normals. It is also limited to diffuse lighting and does not account for specular highlights or ambient occlusion [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Suitable for opaque volume data where visual clarity of internal structures is important and computational resources allow for the extra sampling cost [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Not suitable for transparent volume rendering due to incoherent visual effects, as noted in the source [S1].
 
 ## Background
-This technique is described in the context of comparing GPU-based volume ray casting implementations across Fragment Shader, Compute Shader, OpenCL, and CUDA [S1]. It is presented as a standard method for adding illumination to volume rendering by leveraging the gradient of the scalar field as a proxy for surface normals [S1].
+The technique was described in a 2017 paper comparing GPU-based volume ray casting implementations across Fragment Shaders, Compute Shaders, OpenCL, and CUDA [S1]. The authors implemented diffuse lighting in all four environments to evaluate performance and code complexity [S1]. The approach is based on the standard practice of estimating surface normals from scalar fields using finite differences, a common technique in volume visualization [S1].
 
